    "description": "The recurring errors teams make when they brief content for the AI-search era — from feature-led outlines to unverified sources — and the fixes that make a page quotable.",
    "html": "<h2>Mistake 1: Briefing from features, not questions</h2>\n<p>The default brief lists \"what we want to say\" — product features, brand points. Buyers don't ask about your features; they ask \"how do I do X?\" A feature-led brief produces a page an AI can't map to a query.</p>\n<p>Fix: start the brief from the questions buyers ask, then map your points to them.</p>\n<h2>Mistake 2: No answer hook</h2>\n<p>Teams write a great post but bury the answer three scrolls down. An engine quotes the line it can lift — if your resolution is hidden, it quotes someone else's.</p>\n<p>Fix: put a direct, quotable answer in the first paragraph. The hook is the most-cited sentence; write it like one.</p>\n<h2>Mistake 3: Citing sources you never checked</h2>\n<p>A GEO brief proposes sources. Teams paste them in unverified — and ship a broken link or a source that says the opposite. That hurts trust more than no citation.</p>\n<p>Fix: confirm every source is real, current, and says what you claim before publishing.</p>\n<h2>Mistake 4: Skipping the related questions</h2>\n<p>One query has siblings — \"how,\" \"vs,\" \"best.\" A page that answers only the headline misses the people-also-ask set an engine expects around it.</p>\n<p>Fix: include 5–8 related questions in the brief so the page covers the cluster, not just the keyword.</p>\n<h2>Mistake 5: Treating the brief as the article</h2>\n<p>A brief plans; it does not write. Teams ship the outline plus a thin draft and call it done. The structure helps, but the prose, accuracy, and voice still need a human.</p>\n<p>Fix: use the brief to go faster, then write and edit like the citation depends on it — because it does.</p>\n<h2>Authoritative references</h2>\n<ul><li>Google Search Central: https://developers.google.com/search/docs</li><li>OpenAI: https://openai.com/blog</li></ul>\n",
