    "description": "A repeatable checklist for a GEO-ready content brief — query, outline, related questions, citable sources, and answer hook — and the checks that keep a page quotable.",
    "html": "<h2>Step 1: Anchor the brief to one query</h2>\n<p>Start from a single, real buyer question — \"how do I do X?\" or \"best tool for Y?\" Everything in the brief serves that query. If you can't name the query, the brief isn't ready.</p>\n<p>Pull the query from search-console data, support tickets, or a keyword the buyer uses. Intent first, always.</p>\n<h2>Step 2: Build the outline from sub-questions</h2>\n<p>Map the H2/H3 structure to the query's sub-questions, not to your product sections. A clear outline helps both readers and an engine attribute each point. Aim for one H2 per sub-question.</p>\n<p>A tool that proposes the outline from the query (like BriefKit) keeps this consistent across many briefs.</p>\n<h2>Step 3: Add 5–8 related questions</h2>\n<p>Include the people-also-ask set so the page covers the cluster, not just the keyword. A page that answers the siblings (\"how,\" \"vs,\" \"best\") looks complete to an engine.</p>\n<p>Don't pad — only include questions a real buyer asks.</p>\n<h2>Step 4: List 3–5 citable sources</h2>\n<p>Name authoritative sources the draft can cite. These let an engine attribute claims and trust the page. Critical: verify each source is real, current, and says what you claim before publishing.</p>\n<p>Suggested sources are proposals. The verification is yours.</p>\n<h2>Step 5: Write the answer hook</h2>\n<p>Draft one direct, self-contained sentence that resolves the query — the line an engine is most likely to quote. Put it at the top. If it reads fine out of context, it's a hook.</p>\n<p>Then hand the brief to a writer. The scaffold is done; the prose is human.</p>\n<h2>Authoritative references</h2>\n<ul><li>Google Search Central: https://developers.google.com/search/docs</li><li>OpenAI: https://openai.com/blog</li></ul>\n",
