    "description": "A repeatable method for structuring content so leading LLMs are more likely to quote it — direct answers, citable sources, and a quotable hook, mapped to how answer engines actually work.",
    "html": "<h2>Understand what an answer engine wants</h2>\n<p>ChatGPT and Perplexity don't rank a list; they synthesize an answer. They pull from pages that clearly resolve a query, cite named sources, and state claims plainly. Your job is to make your page the easiest, most trustworthy one to lift from.</p>\n<p>This is closer to \"be the best answer\" than \"win a ranking.\"</p>\n<h2>Structure for the quote, not the scroll</h2>\n<p>Lead with the resolution. Put the answer hook — one direct, self-contained sentence — at the top, then expand. Use clear H2/H3 headings that mirror the sub-questions, so the engine can attribute each point.</p>\n<p>The overlap with good writing is large: clarity and structure help readers and models alike.</p>\n<h2>Cite like you mean it</h2>\n<p>Name 3–5 authoritative sources and link them. An engine can point a reader to where a claim came from, which makes your page safer to surface. Verify each source is real and current — a fabricated or broken citation undermines the exact trust you're building.</p>\n<p>BriefKit proposes the source list; you confirm it.</p>\n<h2>Cover the cluster, not just the keyword</h2>\n<p>One query has siblings: \"how,\" \"vs,\" \"best.\" Include the people-also-ask set so the page matches what an engine expects around the topic. A page that answers the cluster looks complete; a page that answers one keyword looks thin.</p>\n<h2>Authoritative references</h2>\n<ul><li>Google Search Central: https://developers.google.com/search/docs</li><li>OpenAI: https://openai.com/blog</li></ul>\n",
